<BaseLayout title="Human V1 — Connected Training Platform | Wear Valley Digital" description="Human V1 is a connected web and Android training platform designed by Wear Valley Digital for workout creation, plan scheduling, mobile execution and governed synchronization." canonical={canonical} structuredData={structuredData}>
<article class="case-study human">
  <header class="case-hero"><div class="wrap"><nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/work/">Work</a> / Human V1</nav><p class="case-kicker">Connected training platform</p><h1>Human V1 — the athlete’s companion</h1><p class="lede">One connected platform for creating, organising, delivering and completing workouts and training plans across web and mobile.</p><p class="status-line"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>In active product development and private/internal testing.</p></div></header>

  <section class="case-section"><div class="wrap two-col"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">The challenge</p><h2>Building a workout is easy. Preserving its meaning is not.</h2><p>Training often lives across notes, spreadsheets, generic apps, interval timers, PDFs, coaching messages and disconnected calendars. The difficult part is keeping an athlete’s intent and history intact as workouts change, plans are scheduled and unreliable connections interrupt delivery.</p><p>Human V1 needed editable drafts, fixed delivery versions, offline recovery, exact plan dependencies and protected completed history—without making ordinary athletes think like system operators.</p></div><aside class="panel"><p class="quote">A workout must stay understandable to the athlete while remaining exact enough for two devices to agree.</p></aside></div></section>

  <section class="case-section alt"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">The product ecosystem</p><h2>Designed as one platform, not isolated screens</h2><ol class="flow" aria-label="Human V1 product flow"><li><span class="label">01</span><br>Workout Studio<br><small>Create workouts and plans</small></li><li><span class="label">02</span><br>Human V1 platform<br><small>Validate, version and synchronise</small></li><li><span class="label">03</span><br>Human V1 mobile<br><small>Perform training and record progress</small></li><li><span class="label">04</span><br>Trusted status<br><small>Acknowledgement returns to Studio</small></li></ol><p class="section-intro">The responsive Studio handles detailed construction and planning. The Android application keeps training available locally for execution. Shared contracts govern what moves between them.</p></div></section>

  <section class="case-section"><div class="wrap"><div class="architecture"><article><p class="label">Responsive web</p><h3>Workout Studio</h3><p>Structured workout authoring, multi-week plan construction, multiple sessions per day, explicit recovery days and athlete-friendly plan summaries.</p></article><div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↔</div><article><p class="label">Native Android</p><h3>Mobile execution</h3><p>Workout execution, training history, exercise and progress views, local persistence, background synchronisation and Studio content ingestion.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="case-section dark"><div class="wrap two-col"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Synchronization architecture</p><h2>Editable where it should be. Immutable where it must be.</h2><p>Human V1 separates editable drafts from fixed publication versions. A published plan points to exact workout versions. The phone acknowledges the exact revision it applied before Studio describes it as delivered.</p><p>Owner isolation, revision checks, deterministic conflict handling and recoverable queues are built to protect intent. Completed sessions and logged training are not silently rewritten by later edits.</p></div><ul class="feature-list"><li><strong>Canonical models</strong><br>Shared workout and plan structures.</li><li><strong>Exact delivery</strong><br>Version and checksum validation.</li><li><strong>Offline recovery</strong><br>Durable local work and replay.</li><li><strong>Clear status</strong><br>Saved, sending, on phone or needs attention.</li></ul></div></section>

  <section class="case-section alt"><div class="wrap two-col"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Athlete-focused planning</p><h2>From strength sessions to multi-sport schedules</h2><p>The plan model supports structured weeks, multiple daily sessions, priorities and recovery intent. A governed exercise catalogue provides a stable content foundation, while Studio keeps athlete-facing summaries separate from technical delivery detail.</p><p><strong>Research note:</strong> Human V1 includes research-informed candidate training plans designed to demonstrate the platform’s scheduling and multi-sport capabilities. Candidate plans remain subject to sports-science and editorial review and are not individualized medical advice.</p></div><aside class="panel"><p class="label">Governed catalogue</p><p class="metric">955</p><p>exercise records are accepted by the verified catalogue adapter for a complete governed release.</p></aside></div></section>

  <section class="case-section"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Engineering and quality</p><h2>Product engineering across the whole journey</h2><ul class="feature-list"><li>React and TypeScript responsive application engineering</li><li>Native Android and Kotlin product development</li><li>Firebase identity, governed storage and trusted publication services</li><li>Keyboard and focused accessibility verification</li><li>Browser, emulator, domain and recovery testing</li><li>Entitlement and Google Play Billing foundations</li></ul></div></section>

  <section class="case-section alt"><div class="wrap two-col"><div class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Privacy and trust</p><h2>The athlete remains in control</h2><p>Private drafts, schedules, sessions, measurements and account data are not public by default. Human V1 keeps identity and ownership boundaries explicit, preserves history during recovery and requires deliberate action for publication or permanent deletion.</p></div><aside class="panel"><p class="quote">Human V1 explains ordinary product states in ordinary language—and keeps technical detail available only when it helps resolve a problem.</p></aside></div></section>

  <section class="case-section"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Clearly labelled roadmap</p><h2>Protocols, guided intervals and BYOAI</h2><div class="grid"><article class="roadmap"><p class="label">Planned next phase</p><h3>An executable training timeline</h3><p>A visual Protocol Designer and seconds-accurate mobile orchestrator are planned for warm-up, work, rest and recovery steps; rounds, EMOM and AMRAP structures; run/walk, cycling and swimming intervals; audio and haptic cues; and offline execution.</p></article><article class="roadmap"><p class="label">Future concept</p><h3>BYOAI — Bring Your Own AI</h3><p>A privacy-led concept for athlete-controlled AI to explain training, explore plans and prepare governed suggestions.</p><p><strong>AI may propose. Human V1 validates. The athlete approves.</strong></p><p>AI would not silently rewrite history, publish content, change plans, delete training, resolve conflicts or make medical diagnoses.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="case-section dark"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">What this demonstrates</p><h2>From product direction to dependable platform behaviour</h2><p class="lede">Human V1 brings together product strategy, brand and experience direction, information architecture, responsive web engineering, native mobile development, cloud architecture, offline-first data, accessibility and systematic release safety.</p><ul class="tag-list"><li>Product strategy</li><li>UX design</li><li>React + TypeScript</li><li>Android + Kotlin</li><li>Firebase</li><li>Cross-device systems</li><li>Automated testing</li></ul></div></section>

  <section class="case-section"><div class="wrap cta-panel"><p class="eyebrow">A product in progress</p><h2>Human V1 is progressing through internal release acceptance.</h2><p>Workout Studio has an authenticated, entitlement-controlled web presence. The Android application is being validated through private internal testing. Public availability is not yet claimed.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button" href="/legal/contact/">Discuss a digital product</a><a class="text-link" href="/work/">Explore more work</a></div></div></section>
</article>
</BaseLayout>
